  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-10 mb-4">1. Direct Fuel vs. Electricity Cost Comparison</h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    The core financial argument for an electric motorcycle lies in its extraordinary energy efficiency. An electric two-wheeler weighs a fraction of an electric car (typically 75 kg to 130 kg including the battery). Consequently, an electric scooter achieves between <strong>30 to 45 kilometers per single kilowatt-hour (kWh)</strong> of electricity.
  </p>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    Consider a typical daily commute of <strong>35 kilometers (approximately 900 km per month)</strong>:
  </p>

  <!-- Side by side comparison table -->
  <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 bg-white rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 not-prose shadow-xs">
    <table class="w-full text-xs text-left border-collapse">
      <thead class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-950 font-black uppercase text-[11px] border-b border-gray-200">
        <tr>
          <th class="p-3.5">Metric</th>
          <th class="p-3.5 text-red-700">70cc - 125cc Petrol Bike</th>
          <th class="p-3.5 text-emerald-700">Electric Motorcycle (2.5 kWh LFP)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-gray-700 font-medium">
        <tr>
          <td class="p-3.5 font-bold text-gray-950">Efficiency</td>
          <td class="p-3.5">40 - 45 km / Liter</td>
          <td class="p-3.5 text-emerald-700 font-bold">35 - 40 km / kWh (1 Unit)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
